/** siman 467 batch A — chunk 5: turei-zahav (21), magen-avraham (37) */
export const CHUNK5 = {
  "turei-zahav:1:א":
    "Or water fell upon them. In the Tur he wrote: and they have concern for leavening and it is not recognizable in them — end of his words; for if it were recognizable there is no concern, for the buyer will see the leavening:",
  "turei-zahav:1:ב":
    "And one informs him so that he will eat them, etc. In our Gemara it says except one — Rava: let us buy them measure by measure for Israelites so that we consume them before Pesach. And this seif is the language of the Rambam, and his version does not read to Israelites but to a gentile one sells little by little; but to an Israelite one may sell even a large amount once one informs him of the matter — so wrote the Maggid. But the Rosh and Tur have the reading to Israelites, and the author of the Ittur wrote: even if he did not inform the Israelite it is permitted as long as he can eat it before Pesach; and the Rambam wrote that one must inform him, and the Tur wrote, and it is reasonable like the author of the Ittur — from the fact that it is permitted only for a small amount, if one informs him even a large amount is also permitted, etc. Rashal wrote, and one might say: have our mouths been sealed so that we say to the Israelite that it is chametz — why should we rely on the permission that it will be consumed before Pesach not for the need of the matter, since we can inform him? Therefore it appears that it deals with informing, and nevertheless one may not sell more lest he forget and come to a stumbling block — end of his words. And I wonder greatly at the words of the rabbi, for the Rambam holds that one must inform him, and for him it is truly permitted even a large amount, as he states here explicitly; and the Tur's difficulty is according to his reading that reads to Israelites — therefore he questioned even a large amount; but according to what the Maggid said that the Rambam's version does not read to Israelites — the Tur's difficulty is resolved, for if so the large amount is also permitted to an Israelite, only that Rava teaches us that even for a gentile, where informing is not applicable, nevertheless one sells to him measure by measure; and it appears that one should be concerned for the Tur's reading, which is the reading of most books — namely to Israelites, and it is not permitted except with informing; and nevertheless one should not sell except a little, for the reason that when he sells a large amount and will rely on the informing there is concern lest he forget to inform one of the buyers and a stumbling block will come — unlike when he sells a little to a gentile, where there is a double uncertainty that perhaps he will not sell it to an Israelite; and if you say he will sell it to him, perhaps he will eat it before Pesach, as stated above — for practical halachah:",
  "turei-zahav:2:א":
    "For since they rested at ease. The Tur wrote this in the name of R' Eliezer: even though regarding what descends below they are not forbidden unless they split open — that case is different, for there the Torah law is under discussion, therefore they do not come to leavening until they split open; but here, since they rested at ease, they come to leavening; and the Ran in the name of Ra'ah wrote that here splitting open is not required, for it deals with wheat that splits open in its natural growth, and specifically regarding barley splitting open is required; and Ra'am distinguished between small dripping and abundant soaking, as explained in the Beit Yosef; and since he mentioned here the view of R' Efrayim, it implies that we hold there is no distinction between wheat and barley nor between small dripping and abundant soaking.",
  "turei-zahav:2:ב":
    "By force of double uncertainty. It requires further study regarding the Rama's view in this, for he wrote these words — the Agur brought them in the Beit Yosef regarding wheat over which water passed via the roof, that we rely on the permission that Ra'avyah wrote; and the Mordechai brought at length in the name of Ra'avyah to eat some of them before Pesach or to sell to a gentile little by little and the rest is permitted by force of double uncertainty — perhaps water did not descend upon them, and if you say water descended upon them, perhaps they did not leaven (meaning: were not leavened); but the lower wheat that is near the floor of the attic — it is nearly certain that water descended there to the thickness of a finger or half a finger, and those one discards and the rest is permitted to keep; but not to eat from them on Pesach, for even though regarding it shall not be seen uncertainties are nullified in their majority regarding eating, which is in any amount — we are not lenient — end of his words. That which he wrote to eat some of them, etc. — it appears there is an error in his words, for this permission to eat some of them the author of the Or Zarua brought in the name of Ra'avyah in the lengthy Mordechai — that it is permitted even on Pesach, for we can say it is a double uncertainty: perhaps they already ate from the forbidden ones, and if you say they did not eat, one can say they will now eat from the permitted — as with a ring of idolatry that became mixed and one fell into the Great Sea and all were permitted to benefit together, for certainly there is permission; and even if they did not eat from it, one may take from it and throw it into the sea, or eat from them before Pesach, and the rest are permitted; and there is no nullifying prohibition l'chatchila since it is before Pesach, and you have no matzah that is not more than the prohibition — two prohibitions — and in double uncertainty we follow leniency even for d'oraisa prohibition — end of his words. And in the Agur that we mentioned he mixed two reasons for permission — namely to eat some of them, which applies even if there is certainly chametz among them; and he also mentioned double uncertainty from perhaps they did not leaven — and it appears to me there is some leap in his words. And in any case, since the essence of the permission here is by force of double uncertainty, why is it forbidden in eating on Pesach? For even in d'oraisa prohibition it is permitted in double uncertainty — all the more so in rabbinic prohibition in any amount; and further, the Rama himself who wrote here that double uncertainty does not help for eating — he wrote in siman 453 seif 3: if there is grain among them that sprouted — for if there are sixty in what did not sprout it is permitted if one grinds them, and that is even to eat on Pesach — behold they do not say it returns and revives; and why did he rule here forbidden because it returns and revives? And necessarily one must say that here it deals where there are not sixty against the prohibition, and the essence of his emphasis teaches that it is not permitted even in double uncertainty. First difficulty: why does double uncertainty not help here in truth? Further, Ra'avyah truly permitted because of double uncertainty even for eating, as stated in his name, and also in the next seif it is proven thus — it requires study. And for the law, it implies even according to all that the prohibition for eating is because there is no loss in keeping them — it implies that if there is loss, such as when he has no other grain, it is permitted even in eating.",
  "turei-zahav:4:_":
    "We are not concerned for stam, etc. — so wrote the Rashba in a responsum that the Beit Yosef brought, and this is its language: and even those stacks that we saw rain fell upon them — we suspend to leniency that perhaps water did not enter except in the upper sheaves but not in the belly of the stack; and if you say it entered, perhaps it did not have time to leaven as long as it did not come in water — therefore there is double uncertainty, and for leniency — end of his words. From this too there is contradiction to the Rama's ruling nearby, for behold here it is permitted even in eating:",
  "turei-zahav:8:א":
    "There are those who are stringent. It is the Mordechai that we are concerned lest flour or fine flour was mixed in. And the Beit Yosef wrote that there is no prohibition in this, for it is fruit juice and only fine flour leavens; and it is possible that they are concerned that it is full chametz since they knead wheat first. And the Rosh wrote in a responsum general 24 that he did not see a custom of prohibition in honey, for mixing flour is not common and also it was nullified before Pesach; and if we come to be concerned because of mixtures we should forbid it all year — some say they put nevelah meat into it and it turns into honey and is permitted, for after the fact we follow — end of his words. And nevertheless the Rama concludes that one may not eat it on Pesach except what they bring from the hive, even though in the Mordechai he forbids this too because of substitution with other honey — it is an extra stringency. And I saw in many places that they eat it, and also in the holy community of Krakow there is no custom to forbid — unlike the Rama's words here that one should not eat honey except what they bring from the hive that they make a drink from, etc. — meaning even to eat it as is it is permitted, for they make a drink from it l'chatchila; but one should not say that specifically the drink is permitted because it was made before Pesach, for he did not mention before Pesach; and further, he began with eating — therefore this too even in eating is permitted, only that he mentioned the drink as evidence that people practice leniency. And based on this it appears to me there is full permission to cook on chol ha-mo'ed a drink called mead from honey that is recognizable as coming from the hive to the sight of people — anything fit for drinking on Yom Tov; even though the author of the Or Zarua wrote to forbid making the drink on the festival because of concern for mixtures, and wrote that so they practiced — nevertheless it appears to me that one who is lenient for the honor of Yom Tov in these countries where we have no other drink on Pesach has not lost; and there is no applicable custom of prohibition here, for each one prepares for himself before Pesach; but if one comes to cook on the festival and has no drink other than that — one should not protest him, and honoring Yom Tov is in his hand, as appears to me. And based on the words of the Rosh's responsum that we mentioned in the name of R' Yonah, it appears to teach merit on what is written in Shulchan Aruch Yoreh De'ah siman 114 that the Rashba was careful from saffron because they mix dry meat in it; and according to R' Yonah's words there is permission since the saffron dissolves in food and the prohibition is nullified, and it is literally like honey here all year:",
  "turei-zahav:8:ב":
    "And dried figs. For there is concern they scatter flour on them; and even those that grew in his garden are forbidden because of what an observer sees — because of other figs; and the prohibition of grapes is that they are placed near figs:",
  "turei-zahav:8:ג":
    "And tsuker kandil. This is not what we call tsuker kandil — that has more concern and is full chametz, for they cook white tsuker in sour vats and it reddens; and even to keep it is forbidden because there is a mixture of chametz. Rather it is tsuker kandiya that Maharil wrote they ate on the last Yom Tov, and R' Nasan gave the students tsuker he brought from Candia that was clear to them there is no chametz in it. And it is difficult why they did not decree because of other tsuker like with figs; and it appears that tsuker is a distinct species and its form is recognizable, and we do not decree on it; and it appears this is what we call kaner or hot tsuker that certainly has no chametz and no cooking was done on it — only they bring it as its natural product; that is what they eat on the last Yom Tov, as stated:",
  "turei-zahav:8:ד":
    "Saffron that they call zafarin. And even that which they call lond zafarin that is certainly without mixtures is forbidden because of what an observer sees, lest he not know to separate — so I found in an old collection:",
  "turei-zahav:9:_":
    "Even if they did not split open. It implies in any case the matter was softened; and so too the author of the Or Zarua regarding a wheat kernel found in dough:",
  "turei-zahav:10:_":
    "On the eighth day of Pesach, etc. It appears all the more so regarding taste imparted, for we are stringent on Pesach as written in siman 446 — it is permitted to keep it on the last Yom Tov, for it is lenient regarding rabbinic prohibition in any amount:",
  "turei-zahav:11:_":
    "And so they practice during Pesach. The distinction of during Pesach is in the Beit Yosef, who questioned what the Tur wrote in the name of the Rosh regarding a wheat kernel found embedded in dough or matzah — even though permitted, etc., and we are not concerned lest it was in water, because Israelites are accustomed to guard the dough, etc. — it implies that if it had been in water the matzot would be forbidden; and afterward he wrote an incident where an embedded wheat kernel was found in a vessel of water and the water is permitted; and one might say that here it deals during Pesach and that incident deals on the eve of Pesach; and they already questioned on this, for certainly that incident also deals on Pesach as the Tur implies; and I wondered — for according to the Tur, behold the Tur wrote afterward and so Avi HaEzri permitted wheat that fell into a pit, and wrote they do not leaven, etc.; and it appears the case is not comparable to the proof, for that one is specifically in dough, but an embedded wheat kernel certainly leavens in cold water like a stalk that leavens in its husk; but it appears to permit from the Tur because the water is cold the wheat kernel does not impart taste in them, and even full chametz that fell into a pit is permitted for this reason — end of his words; behold the wheat kernel is forbidden in cold and does not forbid — therefore there is difficulty on the Rosh, for if the wheat kernel had been in water it would certainly have been forbidden, and you have no permission in matzah except because the matzah is not forbidden in cold; with this it appears the Rosh holds this is specifically when resting in cold, but in kneading the dough warms somewhat through the work and compresses the wheat kernel in the hand or vessel — certainly there is concern it squeezed some taste from it and forbids; and all the more so in baking; and if so it is not necessary to distinguish between before Pesach and during Pesach. And in Maharil it is written: a wheat kernel was found on the eve of Pesach in matzah — Semak permitted removal of the place; and if a wheat kernel was found on Pesach in matzah before baking, Maharil ruled to burn it by itself in a bonfire; and if they removed the wheat kernel from it and baked it, they must burn it, and the rest of the matzot baked with it he permitted. And a wheat kernel found in dough — the entire dough is forbidden; so ruled Maharash, and Maharshal agreed in responsum siman 79, because through kneading it leavens and gives some taste; and so too the Rama's words here, and so the author of the Or Zarua — if the wheat kernel softened. And what the Rama wrote here that we say here it was found, here it was — the Levush questioned: if the wheat kernel fell there automatically it was not in water and is not forbidden at all — difficulty on the Rama; the Rama's intent here is that we are concerned lest a wheat kernel fell like a chametz kernel from elsewhere into this matzah — here it was found and here it was. And the Levush explained there is prohibition only from the wheat kernel to baked matzah, but in dough he permits because it does not leaven, and wrote the custom is not like the Rama's words to forbid in dough or in matzah that is not baked. And behold in the Tur he compared here dough to baked matzah, and it implies that if it had been in water there would be prohibition; and certainly the author of the Levush did not see the books' words that we mentioned, that forbid even in dough; and the essence is as Magen Avraham wrote here:",
  "turei-zahav:12:א":
    "If they scalded a chicken, etc. Stam scalding is in a second vessel; and so explicitly in Sefer Rokeach siman 211:",
  "turei-zahav:12:ב":
    "The chicken is forbidden. For perhaps the wheat kernel was in water before scalding and leavened and absorbed from the water — so too in the Tur in the name of the Ramban; and it is difficult why we do not say here it was found here it was; and after scalding the chicken it came there. And it appears he derives prohibition from impurity, for we say in chapter Kol Me'arvin (Eruvin page 35): one touched one at night and does not know if alive or dead, and the next day he found it dead — the Sages render impure, for all impurities are as at the time of finding; and for this reason Rokeach wrote: a wheat kernel found in baked matzah and it is unknown if it leavened while still dough or never leavened — that matzah alone is forbidden, but other matzot we establish on their presumption and permit; here too we say the water retroactively as at the time of finding now; and one should not say here too the chicken on its presumption, for since the water was certainly in those and certainly leavens, and the chicken was certainly there — its presumption was impaired, unlike other matzot above, for even though we say retroactively as now, that is only in that matzah, but it is not necessary for other matzot, as appears to me. And one should not say it is double uncertainty that perhaps the wheat kernel was digested in the chicken — as the Rama wrote afterward one can say it deals with a whole wheat kernel; and also one should not say perhaps it did not leaven, since one who is expert can discern — this is not called a sufficient uncertainty, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 84:",
  "turei-zahav:12:ג":
    "An embedded wheat kernel. It implies but if it is not embedded there is no prohibition, for it is double uncertainty — perhaps it did not leaven, and if you say it leavened, perhaps afterward it fell; all the more so here where it is visible that it is not embedded — only we are stringent from uncertainty; but the Rama wrote nearby seif 9 that we forbid in any amount even if they did not split open; and it appears here where there is double uncertainty we permit selling the chicken to a gentile, and we are stringent only after the second uncertainty, like that seif 9:",
  "turei-zahav:12:ד":
    "If it is during Pesach. This is written according to his answer to the Tur's difficulty contradicting itself in seif 11; but according to what is difficult anyway, it is permitted here even on Pesach, for cold does not impart taste at all:",
  "turei-zahav:12:ה":
    "By force of double uncertainty. The author of the Or Zarua questioned that this is not double uncertainty, for the mind of fools is not a mind, and it is possible that one who is expert knows to assess that it was not digested; and I do not know the difficulty, for this matter cannot be known — it is like entities in a full stomach; and all such cases are certainly uncertainty, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 81. And one can say: here in this seif regarding grain on top he wrote the Rama that one may not permit for eating because of double uncertainty; and one should not say here there is another leniency that there is only rabbinic prohibition in any amount, but regarding grain there is uncertainty in all the grain — this is not so, for above too in some places it implies the majority is permitted; and so is the law in that very responsum of the Rashba that the Beit Yosef brings in a year when rains were abundant — what is written in Shulchan Aruch seif 4, and there it is explained that the majority is permitted; and further, wherever double uncertainty helps, it helps even for d'oraisa prohibition; and therefore it appears he was stringent there for eating only because there is possibility without loss — it is like a matter that has permitters; and as the author concluded in that law that we copied in seif 2, concluding: since there is no loss in keeping it, etc. — unlike food needed for Pesach, where we follow practical halachah, as appears to me. Rokeach wrote: a piece of chametz was found in a bucket of water and it was unknown when they took the water from the bucket whether chametz was in it or not, and they cooked in it and they poured out the cooked dish — but the water in the bucket is forbidden, for perhaps the gentile who brings water put chametz in at that hour — end of his words; and the Beit Yosef brought these words and they have no explanation; and it appears there is an error, and it should read: when they took water from the well — the intent is they drew water first from the well to cook, and afterward drew water in a bucket and chametz was found in the bucket, and it was unknown whether chametz was in the well when they drew to cook — automatically they cooked in chametz water; with this he ruled the cooked dish permitted, but the water in the bucket alone is forbidden, for the gentile drawing to the bucket put chametz at that hour, but that chametz was not in the well at all. And it is astonishing on the Beit Yosef who copied and did not notice; and how can it be — difficult, for it implies if the chametz was before it was cooked it would be forbidden, but chametz in cold does not forbid, as above; and it is possible he holds like the Rashba that the Beit Yosef brings, who argues on the Rosh and holds cold also forbids, and we hold like the Rosh and Tur that cold does not forbid; and it appears if chametz soaked in cold water from the eve — forbidden to all, for soaked is like cooked; and so the author of the Or Zarua in the name of Rashal. The Tur wrote, and this is its language in Sefer Rokeach: a wheat kernel found in a pot — the spoon with which they stirred in it is forbidden; if he placed it in another pot, all are forbidden; and the Beit Yosef on this: it is simple, and why did he not copy it in Shulchan Aruch? And I say this requires deep study, for at first glance this passage is not halachah, for the Talmud Yerushalmi siman 93 in the name of Rabbeinu Tam wrote: what is forbidden in any amount does not impart taste, and any amount from it does not forbid in any amount afterward — meaning from what it releases; and as the Tur wrote there: even if forbidden in its species in any amount — for R' Yehuda we do not say two any-amounts; and if so here, where the spoon forbidden from the pot containing a wheat kernel is forbidden because of any amount — why should it forbid another pot in any amount? And so in the Levush siman 92: chametz and yeast forbidden in any amount do not impart taste, and if it fell again into another pot it is nullified in the majority if unrecognized, and if recognized one discards and the rest is permitted — end of his words; and there we wrote that this if unrecognized nullification is not the intent, and they said this only when recognized one discards; and its release is not forbidden in any amount since it itself was forbidden in any amount; and if so this case here is very difficult; and it appears to me to answer properly: here with the spoon we are concerned lest the spoon touched the wheat kernel itself in the pot and was forbidden from it, not from the cooked dish in the pot. And we find such in Yoreh De'ah siman 95 regarding bowls washed in a dairy churn — they forbid there because the bowls touch the griddle and receive taste from it, and we do not follow the water in the griddle; here too the spoon receives from the wheat kernel itself; and one should not say here as well that the way of the spoon is to touch everything in the pot when stirring, and it was forbidden by taste imparted, not by any amount, for there had to be sixty in what was absorbed in the spoon against the wheat kernel; and it is possible there is not so much absorption, even though we assess the whole spoon everywhere — that is for stringency to assess against it because we do not know how much comes out, but for leniency no, for perhaps there is not so much absorption that there would be sixty in it against the wheat kernel — therefore it properly forbids (but if one inserts) another spoon into that pot that was forbidden because of inserting the first spoon — that spoon is not forbidden so as to forbid another pot when they insert it, since it is forbidden only in any amount from the cooked dish, it does not forbid others in any amount; and similarly if they removed the wheat kernel from the pot it fell in and someone inserted a spoon — it does not forbid afterward by insertion into another pot in any amount, unless there is sixty against the spoon, and it is permitted, as appears to me clearly; and this is a peg that will not move in seif 4:",
  "turei-zahav:13:_":
    "Into the pit during Pesach. In the Tur it is written stam, but the Beit Yosef explained it deals during Pesach, for if before Pesach behold it was nullified before Pesach and does not return and revive; and if so Shulchan Aruch holds like the first view in siman 447 seif 4; and the author of the Or Zarua wrote that nevertheless l'chatchila it is forbidden even before Pesach, since it is possible to draw water from another pit; and in my humble opinion here sometimes it is forbidden even b'dieved even before Pesach, for the leavened water remained above — even though a little mixed above, nevertheless they stood and endured and are as if in their own substance, and it resembles that law in R' Yehuda siman 92 regarding did not stir and covered, for it does not mix entirely except if they already drew from there several times before — then it is like stirring the pot and it is nullified before Pesach; but during Pesach there is no distinction, as appears to me. And the Mordechai wrote in chapter 20: this law of hag'alah of a pot also applies; and afterward wrote: but wheat that fell into a pit of water, etc., that it does not leaven while the wheat kernel is cold — end of his words. Once a servant brought a vessel with honey called mead to drink and a wheat kernel was found in it; I said it does not forbid in cold, even though it leavened, as the Tur wrote: since the water is cold the wheat kernel does not impart taste in them, and even full chametz that fell into a pit is permitted for this reason — end of his words. And even though it is written in Shulchan Aruch seif 12 that some forbid during Pesach — we already proved there is no distinction found except the Beit Yosef wrote thus as an answer to explain the Rosh's words, and we are not concerned lest it was in water, etc.; and we already mentioned the difficulty; and if so we do not find anyone who disagrees on this matter; and on the contrary, from the Mordechai we mentioned nearby it is proven that on Pesach itself the wheat was in the well, and nevertheless he wrote it does not impart taste in cold; and also there is no concern here lest the wheat kernel was when the honey was boiling, for then what was released from it was already nullified before Pesach and does not return and revive — even though the wheat kernel is still inside, nevertheless at that time it was cold and does not impart taste, as stated:",
  "turei-zahav:14:א":
    "One peels all of them. In responsum of the Mishna Berurah siman 69 he disagrees on this, that peeling is not required for the rest, for if because of concern lest they touched the wheat kernel itself we do not establish prohibition except here it was found here it was; and he expanded somewhat on this. And it is astonishing on him that he did not examine Shulchan Aruch in Yoreh De'ah siman 105 seif 9 that wrote there: and if it is unknown whether milk touched all of them, all of them, etc. Further difficulty: here peeling is not required at all, since the wheat kernel does not spread at all like a weak thing; and he expanded that we are not stringent on chametz on Pesach to say it spreads. And certainly this is true, as we mentioned in siman 461 seif 4 regarding adhesion to matzah — only that case is different, for there is nothing that spreads entirely, unlike when there is meat that spreads — we are concerned lest it went and seasoned the wheat kernel and it returns to spread in the meat, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 98 seif 5; and even a thin piece of prohibition and the permitted of its kind, etc. — we can say here too with matzah and meat; but I already cast a stone on this passage in Yoreh De'ah there. And I mentioned Rashal's words that in salting at least we do not say this reasoning; and nevertheless peeling is required even for the rest because of concern for contact, as stated; and in that responsum he mentioned there that wherever peeling is required and not possible it is permitted like Ra'avad's view. And I brought bundled proofs in Yoreh De'ah siman 91 that we do not hold like Ra'avad, but sixty is required against the peel:",
  "turei-zahav:14:ב":
    "And there are those who are stringent. The reason we hold in all prohibitions salting is like cooking — so too regarding chametz; and the Rama decided to be lenient in the other pieces, since also in other prohibitions it is only a stringency of the custom that peeling suffices in salting according to most poskim; and therefore on chametz on Pesach, which is rabbinic prohibition in any amount, one may be lenient; and it would have been fit to be lenient even in that piece that peeling should suffice; only since we see the wheat kernel and it rests on it, one should be stringent on it like the stringent ones; and there is an error in what he wrote, etc. — and it should read to sell everything to a gentile, even though we do not practice thus in other chametz mixtures, as in siman 447; and he means that here in salting it is more lenient than other chametz mixtures mentioned in siman 447. And for the law we saw dispute among the early ones in the Mordechai chapter 20 whether to forbid in salting like cooking; and the later ones — Rashal in responsum siman 79 — rule to burn everything, and so too the author of She'erit Yosef and the author of the Or Zarua. And the Mishna Berurah disagrees with him to rule like the lenient ones, and he is lenient in other pieces even without peeling; and the Rama in responsum siman 128 wrote to permit other pieces, and this is its language: even one who wishes to be stringent should rule as he heard some sages instruct to burn that piece and keep the others after Pesach; and in my humble opinion one should practice thus: if a wheat kernel is found on salted dry meat without brine, one may permit keeping everything this way — where it was found one peels, and the rest one keeps, since there is only uncertainty of contact and one does not eat from it on Pesach but keeps it for after Pesach; and this certainly has no stumbling block, since for many great early and later authorities it is eaten, and we do not keep it; but if the wheat kernel is found inside the brine of meat or fish, then it is considered like cooked, and all the more so if it rested there from the eve — its law is as all that rests in the brine is as if cooked in one pot and all is forbidden and requires destruction, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 105 regarding soaked in brine or vinegar that in a small measure it is like cooked; but what rests above the brine one may also keep until after Pesach, for spreading does not go upward in brine, as explained in Yoreh De'ah seif 9 that there is no spreading upward in brine; and therefore what touches the meat in the brine one peels, and the rest one keeps or does as the Rama wrote at the end to sell everything to a gentile; and it appears here too one should peel the place of the wheat kernel first, for this is full chametz, as appears to me; and this is when it is not in the brine, but if it is in brine it is forbidden to sell, for it is full chametz, and one should do as written nearby. And what Shulchan Aruch wrote in this seif: if an embedded wheat kernel is found — it is plain that for us the law is thus if it softened, even though it is not embedded, as stated:",
  "turei-zahav:16:_":
    "The bird is permitted. For roasting does not forbid more than peeling, and the crop is peeling — so too in the Beit Yosef in the name of the author of the Or Zarua; and even though we hold roasting requires sixty, here where spreading in a wheat kernel is not applicable peeling suffices; and further, searing is better than roasting — therefore even for us this bird is permitted:",

  "magen-avraham:1:א":
    "In the Tur it is written: and the chametz is not recognizable in them, etc. — it implies that if the chametz is recognizable it is permitted, and we are not concerned lest he grind and sell them, since buyers do not buy flour from the market except in time of distress, as in siman 453, as stated above, and not as the author of the Shulchan Aruch. And one informs him — but they do not rely on proclamation (Shulchan Gavoah chapter 5 of Avodah Zarah):",
  "magen-avraham:1:ב":
    "So that it will be consumed. Meaning: even if you say he will sell it to an Israelite, the Israelite will eat it before Pesach; and the Kesef Mishneh wrote the explanation: when he buys only measure by measure for his household he needs it and it will be consumed before Pesach — there is nothing to fear — end of his words; and it requires study, for if so why does he say and it will be consumed before Pesach — it implies that if they know it will not be consumed before Pesach, such as selling it on the eve at the fifth hour, it is forbidden; and for the Kesef Mishneh it should be permitted, for he buys it for his household need; and further, in Avodah Zarah chapter 4 seif 5 it says regarding wheat over which rain fell, and Rava permitted selling to a gentile, and Rabah bar Rav Huna challenged him and he retracted and decided to sift and sell to a gentile not in the presence of Israelites (for they do not buy bread from gentiles); and Tosafos questioned: after Rabah already challenged him in the incident in Pesachim, how did he err here a second time? And Tosafos answered: both incidents came before him at once and he ruled in both to sell to a gentile, and Rabah challenged and he retracted — therefore it is difficult why he troubled them there to sift and sell — he should also have ruled to sell little by little to a gentile; and necessarily specifically for chametz little by little helps, for even if you say he will sell to an Israelite, the Israelite will eat it before Pesach; but for other prohibitions little by little does not help, for we are still concerned he will sell them to an Israelite or give as a gift to his Israelite friend; and further, you have nothing smaller than an egg, and nevertheless they do not sell an egg of nevelah or tereifah to a gentile; and it is implied from the Gemara's language and the poskim even one egg b'dieved siman 86, and in siman 84 the Rabbeinu Yosef brings flour that became wormy to throw into the river — it implies there is no remedy. And what the Rama wrote in siman 454 seif 5 regarding yeast that became mixed — nevertheless one may not sell much together lest he return and sell it to an Israelite — it implies little by little there is nothing to fear; behold these matters are taken from the words of R' Yehuda Vein, and I heard and did not understand — for R' Yehuda Vein never intended thus, for there they detect the taste of wine, and how will an Israelite buy from a gentile? Rather his intent is not to sell everything together to a gentile except the value of the yeast in it — see R' Yehuda Vein; and you will find like my words; but his words too require study, for Tosafos chapter 3 of Avodah Zarah only wrote that one need not sell it together except the value of the yeast, and if he wishes he may sell it together; and so too in Yevamot page 81b and end of Avodah Zarah and in Zevachim page 72 — and this is not the place; but for a matter where there is concern lest he return and sell to an Israelite, there is no permission in little by little except for chametz, as stated; and in Avodah Zarah siman 112 seif 1, and so in the name of Rashdam in Yoreh De'ah siman 45 and R' Yehuda Vein in Yoreh De'ah siman 2, and this is its language: and even though there is double uncertainty that perhaps the Israelite will eat it before Pesach, since it is uncertainty made by one's hands it is forbidden — end of his words; and it requires study, for it deals with much chametz; and one can say thus: perhaps the gentile will sell to many Israelites, to each a little — it appears he holds like the Kesef Mishneh's explanation that on little it depends that the gentile will eat it himself; and in Avodah Zarah double uncertainty 57:",
  "magen-avraham:2:א":
    "For since they rested at ease. For specifically regarding what descends below it is not forbidden unless they split open, since it is under discussion:",
  "magen-avraham:2:ב":
    "To leavening. And if it had not yet rested the measure of mil it had not yet leavened (and so in Talmud Yerushalmi siman 123 and in the Agur); and if so, even if it occurred on Pesach, one may sell it to a gentile before it rested the measure of mil. An incident: an Israelite bought wheat from a gentile, and afterward another Israelite came and asked the gentile to sell him wheat too, and he said I have no wheat, for the Jew I sold to is my friend and I sold him the wheat I washed and intended to grind for my household need and sold them to that friend — it appears similar to what is written in Yoreh De'ah siman 16 that a gentile is not believed in what he said after he sold and they left his hand; however if he is believed to him it is forbidden — end of his words; and see there siman 127. The Bach wrote in the Mordechai at length in the name of Ra'avyah: wheat that split open and their splitting is not recognizable that became mixed in a pile — forbidden to keep; and if he transgressed and kept them, they are forbidden after Pesach and forbidden to sell to a gentile; but if they ate from the grain before Pesach, permitted to eat on Pesach, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 110 seif 7 that one may eat the remainder two by two; and even if they did not eat from it, one may take from it and throw into the sea, and the rest are permitted; and there is no matter of nullifying prohibition l'chatchila, for Pesach has not yet arrived (and see siman 447 in the name of the Rashba); and even though one may eat only two by two, one by one is forbidden — here too you have no matzah that is not more than the measure of prohibition — two prohibitions — therefore all is in it he wrote that keeping mixed wheat is permitted; dry with dry is nullified — so the Bach for the law; and what Ra'avyah wrote when some were eaten that the remainder may be eaten on Pesach — because he holds it does not forbid more than removal; but for us who hold it forbids entirely — if so any amount of chametz remaining forbids all the matzah, for it imparts taste in any amount (Bach); and see siman 461; and according to what is written in siman 447 seif 9 that before Pesach it is nullified in the majority — if so permitted to grind before Pesach and nullify in sixty, siman 453 seif 3, and so Yoreh De'ah siman 109; and there is room to study in the Bach — see there. And one may learn that if dry mixed with dry on Pesach and one of them was eaten, it is permitted, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 110; and it is also possible that the third mixture is permitted, as written there; but in moist these permissions do not apply:",
  "magen-avraham:4:_":
    "We are not concerned. For we follow the majority that they do not leaven:",
  "magen-avraham:8:א":
    "There are those who are stringent. For we are concerned lest flour was mixed in; and even though honey is fruit juice, nevertheless the flour is full chametz, for they are accustomed to wash wheat:",
  "magen-avraham:8:ב":
    "Only that, etc., that they make, etc. Explanation: two things are required — specifically from that in the barrel it is permitted to make a drink, for even if there is a little flour in it, it is nullified in sixty at the time of cooking before Pesach; but those they bring in small vessels are forbidden even to make a drink from them; and many times it was clarified to me they mix much flour in them. And to eat honey as is in any case is forbidden, for substitution will come; and so Maharil and Rashal; and forbidden to make the drink on Pesach (Bach, and so in Darkei Moshe); and the Levush wrote, and this is its language: some say specifically the drink called mead is permitted because it has much water and presumably the flour was nullified before Pesach; but honey as is that they cooked before Pesach — even though they were careful from leavening and strained it from the bees in it — forbidden to eat and to put into food; and it appears one who is lenient has not lost and one who is stringent — may he be stringent; and it requires further study. However honey they bring from the hive — all practice leniency, for there is no way to mix anything in it when they draw it from the hive — end of his words; he was precise and wrote they bring from the hive, and did not write they bring in a barrel from the hive, as written, because that is forbidden to eat as is, and only what they bring with small whole combs as they rest in the hive that was not yet crushed is permitted; and some practice to cook honey water until it returns and becomes thick like honey and eat it; and according to the Bach it is forbidden as stated; and all is according to custom:",
  "magen-avraham:8:ג":
    "And dried grapes. Maharil wrote in responsum siman 105 not to eat dried grapes; and if a person wishes to fulfill what is written in Shir HaShirim open the pomegranate, he can fulfill with our grapes — end of his words. And in Maharil it is written one should be careful with thin grapes, for they mix them with raisins. In the holy community of Kalish they practice leniency from old times to buy small dried pears from gentiles and make a drink from them — meaning they pour cold water on them and soak until it becomes a drink; but to cook with the pears they practice prohibition, and also the pears themselves they do not eat; and the vessels with which they draw the drink they practice not to use for anything else. It appears the reason is concern lest a little chametz adhered to them — therefore they do not eat them, but the drink they drink, for that little is nullified in sixty; and even though they soak also on Pesach, nevertheless they were not stringent from uncertainty; and what they practice prohibition to cook them is because through cooking it will impart more taste; and even though there are gentiles who dry pears in an oven after they baked bread in it, nevertheless it is taste imparted by taste of the permitted; and even though the Rama wrote in siman 447 to be stringent on this — here it is uncertainty, for many dried in heat or after the oven was lit — therefore they practiced leniency, as stated, to justify their custom; but in other places they practiced stringency:",
  "magen-avraham:8:ד":
    "Forbidden to keep it. Shach wrote, and this is its language: it requires study, for it appears to me from the Talmud Yerushalmi the reverse — specifically small combs made from kneading tsuker are forbidden, but stam tsuker is permitted to keep; and so I saw my teacher rule that tsuker that passed over Pesach he permitted — end of his words; and so in Hagahot Maimon: Maharil wrote in responsum siman 25: tsuker — they are accustomed to be careful if it is not great need for healing, for merchants mix many types in it — end of his words; and those made like small combs from tsuker kneading are forbidden to keep (in the name of Maharil and Moharash); and in Talmud Yerushalmi it is written one should be careful in all types of spices made from tsuker kneading, like legumes and coated seeds — end of his words; Hagahot Maimon implies he holds stam tsuker is permitted to keep; and so in the Levush — see there; and so I heard from the craftsman who prepares it that that made like a hat has no flour (and so the author of the Shulchan Aruch); however l'chatchila they practice to sell it to a gentile before Pesach, and b'dieved it is permitted; and Mahari Bruna wrote in a collection: I found a matter in taste that is the reverse — white tsuker has no concern for leavening, and tsuker kandiya has concern for leavening; and so I heard practiced in the holy community of Krakow — end of his words; and Maharil: Maharaz permitted his students to eat on the last Yom Tov tsuker he brought from Candia that was clear to him there is no concern for leavening — end of his words:",
  "magen-avraham:8:ה":
    "Saffron. In responsum of the Rashba siman 133 it is written they are concerned for flour with water and pour on it; and the Levush wrote: what grows in his house garden is forbidden because of a decree (Maharil) — meaning because saffron is not common to grow in these countries, and they will think it is saffron that comes from afar; but fruits a Jew dried we are not concerned for substitution because it is common; and so with honey from the hive, as stated above — therefore Mahari Cohen; and Tuv Ta'am: the way is to soak it in beer and one must seal it in a room or make a partition before it — end of his words; and if so, the same for saffron:",
  "magen-avraham:8:ו":
    "Their mixture does not forbid. It implies even if much mixed in a cooked dish it is permitted, since the concern is distant; however in honey Darkei Moshe wrote, and this is its language: however it appears that if a little of it mixed in a cooked dish on Pesach one does not forbid it, for it is fit to rely on the Rosh in such a case, as in siman 447; and regarding rabbinic prohibition in any amount we do not establish prohibition b'dieved — it implies if mixed in less than sixty it is forbidden, and so is essential; but in saffron and the like one should not be stringent even in less than sixty, for the taste of chametz already passed and went, as in siman 447 regarding dry meat, and Yoreh De'ah siman 69 regarding blood:",
  "magen-avraham:9:א":
    "If they did not split open. For since there is a dispute in the Gemara — some say permitted without splitting open, and rabbinic prohibition in any amount — we follow uncertainties to leniency (Rif); it implies if mixed in less than sixty it is forbidden — see Yoreh De'ah siman 98; and Hagahot Maimon questioned: behold in seif 2 he ruled even if they did not split open it is forbidden; and one might say specifically to eat wheat kernels as they are, but to forbid the cooked dish they were not stringent; and it is difficult, since they are lenient only because of dispute, and necessarily they disagree only regarding what descends below, but where they rested at ease all agree it is forbidden — if so why were they lenient in the cooked dish? And one can say: he wrote above it comes to leavening — it implies they will come to leavening but are not yet certainly chametz, only uncertainty — therefore they permit the cooked dish; see Tur. The Beit Yosef wrote in the name of Kol Bo: if three barley kernels are found in a cooked dish, presumption there is much and perhaps they split open (Darkei Moshe); and it appears to me they omitted this intentionally in Shulchan Aruch, for we do not hold thus, for the Taz wrote in Yoreh De'ah siman 84 seif 9 in the name of Rashal that we do not say thus except regarding worms that multiply from their kind, but regarding a matter that comes from outside no; and further, one can check if barley is in it; however in a pile of wheat over which rain fell, one can say this reasoning — see there seif 2:",
  "magen-avraham:9:ב":
    "They forbid everything. The cover and the spoon — and similarly other cooked dishes mixed from that cooked dish; and so they practice (Bach); and so the Tur in the name of Rokeach: a wheat kernel found in a pot — the spoon with which they stirred in it is forbidden; if placed in other pots, all are forbidden — end of his words; and so siman 12; and the Taz in Yoreh De'ah siman 92 wrote that if a piece was forbidden from chametz in any amount and fell into another pot, it does not forbid through its release — see there; and he was excessive in his measures that everywhere they practice to forbid; and so Mahari Cohen in Hagahot Orach Chayyim of the Rama; and even though the piece does not impart taste, nevertheless regarding the prohibition it forbids in any amount; and there are ramifications, such as a thing made for its taste is not nullified, and where it is not made for taste it is nullified — chametz within its time in any amount, and after its time in sixty; or regarding if mixed in its species and not in its species — we say it ascended from it as if it is not — end of his words; and so the Bach; and see in the Drisha — nevertheless what he wrote chametz within its time, etc. — explanation: if any amount of chametz mixed in a cooked dish during Pesach and he kept it until after Pesach and mixed from that cooked dish into another cooked dish, sixty is not required, only against the any amount; and it is difficult, for the Bach himself wrote in siman 447 that a thing forbidden in any amount that passed over Pesach is permitted without mixture; and in Sefer Nikudat HaKesef it is written: and if it was intentional — meaning when it passed intentionally it is forbidden; but according to siman 447 even if intentional it is permitted — see there; also what he wrote siman 55 that chametz b'dieved forbids in its species in any amount because it is deishilam — the Talmud Yerushalmi already decided it is not deishilam; and so siman 461 regarding double matzah; also what he wrote dry with dry is not nullified on Pesach because deishilam, and not in its species is nullified in sixty — he did not examine the Mordechai that wrote explicitly the reason: as moist in any amount, so dry; and so Yoreh De'ah, and so Talmud Yerushalmi; and if so even not in its species it is not nullified; and even a piece forbidden because of any amount that became mixed with others dry — all are forbidden:",
  "magen-avraham:10:_":
    "Permitted to keep it. Because it is uncertainty of Yom Tov uncertainty of weekday (Tur); explanation: and rabbinic prohibition in any amount is rabbinic — therefore we are lenient to keep it; but full chametz even on the last Yom Tov is forbidden, as in siman 448; and also Hagahot Maimon who wrote the reason because it is rabbinic — they did not intend to be lenient except on rabbinic prohibition in any amount, siman 447 and Beit Yosef:",
  "magen-avraham:11:א":
    "The entire dough. The Rashba siman 176 wrote: not in one place alone did the wheat kernel touch, but here and there through the rolling of the dough; and one should not say here it was found here it was (seif 12); and further the water in the dough carries what comes out from that grain throughout the dough — they did not say peeling except dry with dry; and so even in a place of moisture, most of the dough is like circumcision at the time of circumcision, but in a place of gravy they said gravy carries even a thing that does not spread; and so bread found in dough — the bread is apt to crumble and crumbs mixed in the dough, aside from that it itself may leaven others even though it is baked, only my mind does not lean that a small thing leavens all the dough, etc. — see there; it is proven he holds it forbids even in cold — see seif 12:",
  "magen-avraham:11:ב":
    "Here it was found here it was. Explanation: we say now it fell into this matzah and was never in the dough; and if so, if the wheat kernel is full of water and moist through and through, it appears it was in water and in dough — if so one must forbid all the dough; and nevertheless one can say perhaps it was elsewhere swallowed from water and fell now into the matzah. And it appears all is according to the matter: if there is something to rely on, we rely — forever we rely on the common, whether to be lenient or stringent; and it is not called uncertainty except when there is equal reliance on this and that, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 55; and the Bach wrote, and this is its language: perhaps now it fell, and if you say it was before, perhaps it did not leaven — only if softened it is forbidden, but if not softened all is permitted — end of his words; and see seif 12. Mordechai Margaliot wrote in a responsum: one took cabbage in a bowl from a barrel and brought to another house, and there a wheat kernel was found on it — he permitted the cabbage in the barrel, for we say here it was found here it was — end of his words; but if a wheat kernel was found in the barrel, the cabbage in the bowl is also forbidden, for we say perhaps the wheat kernel was before in the barrel, as in seif 12 regarding the chicken; and similarly one who has dough in hand, cut matzot from it and gave to others, and one matzah remained in his hand and a wheat kernel was found on it — all are forbidden, for we say perhaps it was on it before; for specifically from place to place we do not establish prohibition; and according to the Bach who considers it single uncertainty — if so always permitted for this reason; and it appears to me this uncertainty did not leaven is not uncertainty of lack of knowledge, as the poskim wrote in Yoreh De'ah:",
  "magen-avraham:11:ג":
    "Only the amount of removal. Even in matzah — and see siman 461:",
  "magen-avraham:12:א":
    "The chicken is forbidden. For we say perhaps the wheat kernel was in water at the time of scalding — we do not say here it was found here it was, except from place to place or from piece to piece, as in siman 11; but in one place, such as scalding, where there is uncertainty in time and place — perhaps the wheat kernel was in water before — we do not say now it fell, for so we find chapter 1 of Chullin and chapter 1 of Niddah that we do not say now it deteriorated where there is no presumption; however what the Rabbeinu Yosef wrote in the name of the Mordechai: if a grain of wheat is found in a bowl near the chicken it is permitted because of double uncertainty (as the Rama wrote in the gloss) and not permitted because of here it was found here it was; and one can say: since it is common to find wheat kernels in chickens that eat wheat, we say perhaps it was in the chicken, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 57 we rely on the common — therefore it is permitted only because of double uncertainty — end of his words; Mishna Berurah siman 69 — and it is difficult, for if so even double uncertainty is not, for a common thing is not uncertainty at all, for even for leniency we rely on it, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 57, and so Orach Chayyim HaShalem; rather it must be specifically there it is very common, but a somewhat common thing we consider double uncertainty, as in siman 84; and if so the Mishna Berurah should not have brought from siman 57; and see in the author of the Shulchan Aruch siman 6 in the Beit Yosef that an uncommon thing is not considered double uncertainty; and it appears to me: even though it is common to find wheat kernels in chickens, when they scald them nothing remains inside, especially for Pesach need — therefore it is not called regular uncertainty; and that the Mordechai did not permit because of here it was found here it was, and in the bowl it fell — because the Mordechai holds a second vessel is forbidden on Pesach, as in siman 447 seif 3, and from time to time we establish prohibition; and if you say it was in the bowl when they turned the chicken into it, it was forbidden — therefore he permitted because of double uncertainty that now it is cold and does not forbid — it deals where there was no gravy there:",
  "magen-avraham:12:ב":
    "And there is not enough to impart taste. It appears thus he says: the chametz is minimal and does not impart taste in the water — there is sixty in the water against it — therefore permitted; for since rabbinic prohibition in any amount we are not stringent in cold; and nevertheless some forbid, etc. And the Levush expanded to permit in cold, and wrote in all places he passed he saw great sages and elders who did not forbid in cold — end of his words; and Mahari Cohen that the Gaon Maharal of Prague ruled to permit, but Maharash of Austria wrote to Maharash of Lublin to forbid, and so most of the world practices, and in Krakow; and it appears it depends on custom — end of his words. And in truth, even though the Levush justified the Tur's words not to forbid in cold, behold the Mordechai forbade in roasting meat even if the wheat kernel was found after roasting — see there; and it is possible he forbade because there was gravy, as in siman 447; and so the Beit Yosef here in the name of geonim, and in siman 12 in the name of the author of the Or Zarua — see there; therefore it appears to me regarding meat where there is gravy it is forbidden by law in cold; and similarly if there is concern it dripped from above, also forbidden by law, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 105; and in other cold cases it depends on custom or according to the matter of time of distress; and see seif 11 that in dough one should be stringent, and the same in dough because through kneading it leavens and imparts its taste throughout:",
  "magen-avraham:12:ג":
    "And there is one who permits, etc. It appears from his words he holds the Rabbeinu Yosef forbids in cold even if found after removing the meat and it is not necessary:",
  "magen-avraham:12:ד":
    "For we say perhaps afterward. The language is not precise — how is it permitted from uncertainty? Rather the reason: we establish presumption and say now it fell — so R' Yehuda Vein; and the Beit Yosef and R' Yehuda Vein wrote in the name of the Rashba: if a wheat kernel is found in a vessel in which they rinsed the chicken, we say here it was found here it was, like blemishes, and we do not say it came from the chicken; and so he ruled practically — end of his words; and it is difficult, for the Rashba ruled if a wheat kernel is found in matzah or dough they forbid the matzah or dough, as in siman 11, and we do not say now they fell; and it is difficult according to the Rama's explanation why he wrote we do not say it came from the chicken, etc. — he should have written we do not say it was before in water; and further it is not comparable to blemishes — therefore it appears to me it deals: they salted the chicken and afterward rinsed it in a vessel in cold, and afterward a wheat kernel was found in the vessel — there is concern it was in the chicken and was salted with it; for this he wrote we are not concerned, and brings proof from blemishes: we say in chapter 6 of Ketubot blemishes found in her father's domain — we say presumably there were blemishes already before she was betrothed; and if found in the husband's domain we say now they arose; here too, since the wheat kernel was found in the vessel we do not say it was in the chicken, for that is from place to place; but in matzah or dough it is from time to time, as written above; and if so there is no dispute at all — so it appears to me essentially:",
  "magen-avraham:12:ה":
    "By force of double uncertainty. Perhaps now it fell, and if you say it was before in the chicken, perhaps it was digested; and those who disagree hold specifically for impurity digestion helps, not for prohibition, as written in Yoreh De'ah end of siman 83 — see there in Beit Yosef and so there in Shulchan Aruch; and in the Agudah in Bekhorot it is written: even for the answer of R' Meir of Pontremoli one may not permit a wheat kernel in a chicken, for presumably it swallowed it whole and it is not digestion — see there; it implies even if it broke afterward it is forbidden:",
  "magen-avraham:12:ו":
    "And they roasted chickens there. It should read: not specifically chickens were mentioned, for here it deals with a whole wheat kernel that does not require digestion; if so there is no distinction between chickens and meat — if the wheat kernel is found after removing the meat from there, they do not forbid the meat:",
  "magen-avraham:12:ז":
    "That dripped into water. Explanation: even though the wheat kernel was found inside while the meat was soaking in it, nevertheless it is rabbinic prohibition uncertainty, for some permit in cold — therefore one may permit other cooked dishes:",
  "magen-avraham:12:ח":
    "Or was scalded. Explanation: and afterward the wheat kernel was found — one can say now it fell; and that which he ruled above seif 2 forbidden to eat because of double uncertainty — that is to eat the wheat kernels themselves, but the mixtures are permitted; and if so, if there is double uncertainty in matzah, forbidden to eat it. The Bach wrote: in single uncertainty even to keep is forbidden, even on the intermediate days; and in double uncertainty permitted to keep, and all the more to sell to a gentile, and not for eating that perhaps it was digested — this is not full uncertainty; some say digestion does not help in prohibition, as above; and specifically full chametz such as bread or dough, but a wheat kernel found in water — the cooked dish cooked in it before is permitted, for double uncertainty: perhaps now it fell, and if you say before it fell, perhaps it did not soften until now, and without softening there is no forbidding at all even according to our custom — therefore; and it is implied from his words that even in hot one does not forbid without softening — see there. An incident: one took into his mouth a piece of matzah and a piece of chicken and ate both together, and felt in his mouth there is a wheat kernel and removed the food from his mouth and saw a wheat kernel, and it is not recognizable if it was in the matzah or in the chicken — the chicken may be permitted by double uncertainty: perhaps now it fell to the chicken in cold, and if you say before, perhaps it was digested; but if it was in the matzah — necessarily it was inside at the time of its baking, for if it fell on it in cold it would roll and descend; and nevertheless it appears to me the matzah is also permitted, since rabbinic prohibition in any amount we suspend to leniency that it was in the chicken, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 111 regarding two pots: in one there is sixty — we rely on that which has sixty, and both are permitted; and nevertheless the amount of removal is forbidden by law from that matzah from the place he took the piece; and further it appears to me even a piece of meat is permitted, for we do not establish prohibition from place to place, and we say now it fell on the meat while still cold — if so the matzah is also permitted; but if the meat rested in a bowl and he ate from it, the meat is forbidden, as stated, for from time to time we establish prohibition — perhaps it was in the bowl when they turned the meat into it; and a second vessel forbids on Pesach; and if we say there is double uncertainty in the matzah and you say in the meat — perhaps now it fell — this is not uncertainty, for if you say it was in the matzah you must forbid the matzah, and we do not say this even for rabbinic prohibition, even in double uncertainty; and so Darkei Moshe siman 139; and if so the meat and matzah are forbidden; however according to the majority it is recognizable if the wheat kernel was cooked or baked or not — see Niddah page 3b regarding a box where a rodent was found, it implies if they cooked a cooked dish in a pot and removed it from the fire to another place and it congealed and a wheat kernel was found on top — we say now it fell, for we do not establish prohibition from place to place:",
  "magen-avraham:13:א":
    "One-day-old. And it deals before its time of prohibition (Darkei Moshe); see siman 422:",
  "magen-avraham:13:ב":
    "During Pesach. And the Bach wrote that even if it became mixed before Pesach it is forbidden, even though it is taste imparted by taste of the permitted; nevertheless in water it is not permitted for this reason, for easily he can find water; and so Yoreh De'ah siman 95; and it is possible even the Rabbeinu Yosef agrees to this, only before Pesach it is nullified in sixty:",
  "magen-avraham:13:ג":
    "Because they became mixed. Explanation: even for those who permit cold, here it is forbidden because they became mixed — see siman 447 seif 5:",
  "magen-avraham:14:א":
    "Rinsing suffices. For cold without gravy is permitted to all — see seif 11:",
  "magen-avraham:14:ב":
    "After salting. Explanation: while he was still salting; but if found after he salted and rinsed, we say now it fell, for if so it had been on it before it would have fallen in the rinsing when he rinsed in water; and specifically found on the chicken, but if found inside the chicken certainly it was before; and see Niddah page 3 regarding a box that has rims:",
  "magen-avraham:14:ג":
    "One peels all of them. It should read: the wheat kernel was found on the chicken — there is concern perhaps it touched all of them; but if found inside the chicken the others do not require peeling:",
  "magen-avraham:14:ד":
    "And there are those who are stringent, etc. Because we assess in all prohibitions in salting at sixty, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 105, because we are not expert between weak prohibition and fat — if so also in chametz it forbids entirely:",
  "magen-avraham:14:ה":
    "But the essential ruling, etc. Because he holds we do not say in rabbinic prohibition that prohibition goes from piece to piece through salting; and what they assess in other prohibitions at sixty is because of the matter that ceased, that we need not assess between fat and weak — with this they are lenient, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 105; if so also in chametz sixty suffices; and further, specifically in a prohibition where greasiness applies do they assess at sixty, but not in chametz where spreading does not apply; and further, where there is another side to permit, they rely on the question that chametz is nullified in sixty — you have no greater side of permission than that many geonim hold taste does not spread in salting; and one should make a compromise to forbid that piece, as in seif 11 to forbid that matzah and no more; and one who wishes to be stringent should rule to burn that piece and keep the others resting with it in the barrel; and further one may permit other pieces through benefit by redemption, as in siman 447 seif 1 — end of Rama in responsum siman 128; and so the Mishna Berurah siman 69; and he ruled if the others were cooked without peeling they are permitted, for it is only a stringency to peel them, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 91 — end of his words; and nevertheless it appears to me the piece requires peeling, only the peel does not forbid the piece even if cooked with it; and so is his intent in Yoreh De'ah there, as stated; and examine there that not like the later ones wrote. Also what the Mishna Berurah wrote here even without peeling is permitted — it appears specifically where there is no concern perhaps it touched the other pieces; but if there is concern perhaps it touched the other pieces, by law all are forbidden, as in Shulchan Aruch in Yoreh De'ah siman 105; and even if they know certainly it touched only one and we do not know which — by law all are forbidden and require peeling, for chametz on Pesach is not nullified in the majority, as in siman 447 seif 9; and if so if cooked without peeling it forbids in its mixture, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 69 and siman 98 — see there; specifically in the lower law the lenient prevails, for it is only a mere stringency, for at first they said in the Gemara peeling is not required, only they are concerned lest it absorbed a crumb — therefore the peel does not forbid the piece; but where peeling is required by law, if cooked without peeling it forbids the piece; and with this all the later ones' complaints on the Rama are resolved; and so in the Drisha. And it is written in responsum of Maharam of Lublin: a wheat kernel resting in a barrel of salted cabbage — the entire barrel is forbidden, for soaked is like cooked — end of his words; and it should read it deals where the brine rises over it; and if the brine does not rise over it, it has the law of salting, as in the Rama's responsum; and see siman 447 seif 5; and so I heard a wheat kernel was found in a barrel of fish salted for that purpose, and the Gaon R' Binyamin Av Beit Din of the holy community of Pozna commanded to throw that row into the river and the rest to sell to a gentile; and Mordechai Margaliot further wrote: before Pesach a full crop of wheat kernels was found in salted brine — even though the Rama ruled in Yoreh De'ah siman 105 that chametz on Pesach forbids only the amount of peeling, nevertheless I was not satisfied to permit that chicken, since there was much chametz there, etc. — end of his words; and the Bach's view is like the stringent in salting on Pesach, and so in Shiltei Gibborim; and in Darkei Moshe he wrote he heard in Krakow some are stringent and therefore did not wish to rule on this neither prohibition nor permission — end of his words; and the Mishna Berurah wrote he was in Krakow and did not hear they are stringent on this — therefore; and it is possible the Rama leaned toward that elder author of Shiltei Gibborim:",
  "magen-avraham:15:א":
    "One cuts the place of its spreading. Question: behold he wrote in siman 447 seif 1 and there is no spreading thing, etc. — it implies if there is a spreading thing it forbids entirely; and it should read here too it deals that the chicken is weak:",
  "magen-avraham:15:ב":
    "According to how they turn, etc. It requires study, for it is not so in Yoreh De'ah regarding other prohibitions; and one can say: even though it does not spread much, it spreads a little; and in other prohibitions it is nullified in sixty:",
  "magen-avraham:15:ג":
    "Those touching one another. But if they do not touch one another they do not forbid, and we do not say absorption goes throughout the vessel, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 92 — there it deals with cooking; and if so if the chicken of its species even if they do not touch they forbid everything, as stated; and see siman 491 seif 12:",
  "magen-avraham:16:_":
    "The bird is permitted. For roasting does not forbid more than peeling, and the crop is peeling — therefore one discards it; and the Bach wrote: according to what they practice to forbid in roasting, everything is forbidden — end of his words; and it appears to me in searing all agree it is permitted, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 68; and this is its language of Maharil: one of Maharash — women practice on Pesach to remove the crop before searing it, for they think searing is somewhat close to roasting, and if a wheat kernel is found in it there would be some concern for leavening; and some are not concerned — end of his words; and it is known Maharil holds to forbid entirely in roasting, and nevertheless he is lenient in searing b'dieved; it is written in Sefer Tanya: all matters of Pesach should not be done through a gentile:",
};
